  {"id":"pt5-q001","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. This team added a prompt-based quality gate that classifies draft report findings before publication. One category, \"unsupported market claim,\" now produces many false positives on well-cited synthesis paragraphs, while other categories such as missing citation and source conflict are performing well. Engineers have started ignoring the entire quality report. What should the team do next?","options":["Temporarily omit the noisy category from the active prompt, keep accurate categories enabled, and rebuild it with examples, definitions, and precision/recall evals.","Keep the noisy category active, add a warning that false positives are expected, and ask engineers to manually disregard those findings.","Disable every quality category for a release cycle, collect no automated findings, and re-enable all categories after prompt edits.","Force the quality gate to return strict JSON, reject malformed outputs, and use schema compliance as the main trust metric."],"explanation":"When a single high false-positive category undermines confidence in an automated review system, the practical move is to separate the noisy signal from the useful signal . Anthropic's documented moderation pattern uses application-defined category lists in prompts, and those lists are customizable, so temporarily omitting or suppressing one category is an application-level control rather than a special built-in category toggle. The category should not simply disappear forever. Treat it as a classification problem: collect examples that should and should not be flagged, include edge cases, define the category more precisely, and evaluate with metrics that balance false positives and false negatives, such as precision, recall, and F1. Structured JSON output is useful for reliable parsing, but it cannot by itself make the category judgment correct. Relevant documentation: Content moderation , Classification , and Evaluation tool . This restores trust by removing the category that is currently polluting the review signal while preserving useful automated feedback from categories that are working. Rebuilding the category with positive and negative examples, clearer definitions, and classification metrics addresses the underlying prompt quality problem instead of abandoning the category permanently.","domain":4,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q002","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. During a PR review run, Claude calls your submit_review_findings tool with JSON that is syntactically valid and matches the tool schema. Your CI validator rejects the execution because one finding references a file not changed in the PR and another violates an internal review policy. What should the integration do next to give Claude the best chance to correct the review output?","options":["Publish the valid findings and silently discard rejected entries before completing the run.","Add a broad CLAUDE.md instruction that review findings must satisfy internal policy.","Send an immediate tool_result for that tool_use_id with is_error true and the concrete validation failures.","Retry the request with a higher temperature and ask Claude to produce a cleaner review."],"explanation":"When a tool execution fails after Claude has already emitted a tool_use block, the documented feedback path is a tool_result block that references the original tool_use_id . Marking the result with is_error: true and including concrete validation details gives Claude a direct observation it can use in the next turn, which is the practical retry-with-error-feedback pattern for tool workflows. This is different from relying on broad prompt edits or random retries. Structured output mechanisms such as JSON outputs and Strict tool use help avoid many schema-level failures, but they do not automatically validate CI-specific semantics such as whether a review comment targets a changed file or follows an internal policy. For those application-level checks, your code should validate the result, return specific failure information through the tool-result channel, and let Claude attempt a corrected call. Tool-result ordering also matters in production integrations: tool_result blocks must immediately follow the assistant message that contained the corresponding tool_use , and any tool_result blocks in that user message must come before text. See Handle tool calls , Implement tool use , Structured outputs , and Strict tool use . This uses the documented tool feedback channel to tell Claude exactly why the tool execution failed. Specific validation failures give the model actionable information for a corrected follow-up tool call, while preserving the link to the original tool_use_id.","domain":4,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q003","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. For a nightly backlog triage run, this team plans to submit thousands of unresolved conversations through the Message Batches API. The batch inputs include client tool definitions for MCP-backed customer and order systems that are implemented by the team’s service. Which design is most appropriate?","options":["Use batch for initial analysis or tool requests, then execute returned client tools and continue each case in later Messages requests.","Prompt Claude to infer missing customer and order fields whenever a batch item cannot complete the tool loop.","Move all customer support tools into batch requests as server tools so refunds can complete without follow-up requests.","Include the client tool schemas in each batch item and let the batch worker execute the backend tools before finalizing responses."],"explanation":"The important distinction is not that Message Batches cannot use tools at all. Anthropic documents that batches can include many Messages API features, including tool use, and that server tools can run as part of batch processing. For client tools , the lifecycle is different: Claude returns stop_reason: \"tool_use\" with one or more tool_use blocks, then the developer's application executes the tool and sends a later user message containing tool_result blocks. A single batched request cannot contain that full application-mediated loop, because the batch result is retrieved after processing rather than interactively supplying tool results mid-request. In practice, use batches for latency-tolerant first-pass analysis, extraction, or the first turn that may request client tools. After downloading results, correlate outputs by custom_id , execute any requested client tools in your application, and submit follow-up Messages requests, either synchronous or batched depending on latency needs. See Message Batches API , Handle tool calls , and Tool use overview . This design matches the client-tool lifecycle and the asynchronous nature of batch processing. A batch item can return a tool_use block, but your application must retrieve that result, run the client tool, and send tool_result blocks in a follow-up request before Claude can use the tool output.","domain":4,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q004","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. During a PR review run, the main session reads the changed files, notes a failing test, and then delegates to an ordinary named security-review subagent, not a fork. The subagent returns generic findings and misses PR-specific evidence from the diff and test output. What change would most directly improve the review's reliability?","options":["Include the changed files, key diff excerpts, failing test output, and review criteria in the subagent prompt.","Start a fresh named subagent invocation and rely on its persisted transcript from earlier CI steps.","Compact the main conversation before delegation so the subagent inherits only the important parent context.","Instruct the subagent to read the parent conversation transcript before producing review findings."],"explanation":"Ordinary named Claude Code subagents are designed for isolated, task-specific work . That isolation is valuable in CI because a security reviewer can have its own instructions, tool access, and permissions, while the main review conversation avoids being flooded with every intermediate step. The tradeoff is that the subagent does not automatically know what the main session already read or decided. For a non-fork subagent, the practical handoff pattern is to put the needed evidence directly in the delegation prompt: changed files, important diff excerpts, failing test output, relevant assumptions, and the review criteria. Without that explicit context, the subagent may fall back to generic review patterns, which increases false positives and misses PR-specific issues. A fork is the documented exception because it inherits the parent conversation, but the situation uses an ordinary named subagent. Compaction, CLAUDE.md guidance, and separate persisted transcripts can be useful for other context-management problems, but they do not replace a complete task message for a fresh non-fork subagent. Learn more in Claude Code subagents . This directly addresses how ordinary named subagents receive task-specific context. A non-fork subagent starts with its own context, so the delegation prompt must carry the PR facts it needs to perform a grounded review.","domain":1,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q005","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. During an evaluation run, the coordinator's first Messages API response contains a brief text block plus two Task tool_use blocks, and the run history shows no subsequent tool_result messages before report generation. The report is consistently based only on the coordinator's initial text. What change should you make to the coordinator loop?","options":["Limit the coordinator to three iterations, summarize partial findings, and stop whenever the iteration cap is reached.","Forward the coordinator's text block to synthesis, skip Task execution, and end the turn when any assistant text is present.","Send each subagent output as a separate tool role message, then continue only if the coordinator asks in prose.","Run each requested Task tool call, send matching tool_result blocks, and keep calling the Messages API until stop_reason is end_turn."],"explanation":"In a client-executed tool loop, stop_reason is the control signal. A response with stop_reason: \"tool_use\" means Claude has emitted one or more structured tool_use blocks and expects the application to execute those requested tools, then return matching tool_result blocks. It is not a final-answer state, even if the assistant response also includes a short text block. The normal successful exit condition is stop_reason: \"end_turn\" , which means Claude has finished its response naturally and the application should use the final assistant response. Tool results must be integrated into the ordinary message history: assistant messages contain tool_use , user messages contain tool_result , and each result is matched with the original tool_use.id through tool_use_id . This design keeps Claude responsible for deciding what information it needs next, while the application remains responsible for executing tools and maintaining the loop correctly. See Anthropic's guidance on handling stop reasons and the tool loop described in how tool use works . The formatting rules for returning tool results are covered in handle tool calls . This implements the documented client-tool loop: a tool_use stop reason means Claude is requesting tool execution, not giving the final answer. The application must execute each requested tool, return matching tool_result blocks, and continue until Claude naturally completes with end_turn.","domain":1,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q006","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. This team is adding a custom MCP server and client-side integration for engineering reference material. Developers want the host to present a browsable list of design docs, runbooks, database schemas, and architecture notes, then attach selected content to Claude's context only when needed. They do not want Claude to spend turns calling exploratory functions just to discover what exists. What design best matches MCP's control model?","options":["Add a list_reference_material tool, require Claude to call it first, and fetch selected documents through additional tool calls.","Publish each reference item as a prompt, require developers to run a slash command, and inject the document text from that prompt.","Copy the catalog into CLAUDE.md, refresh it during repository updates, and ask Claude to request document names from developers.","Declare the server's resources capability, expose catalog metadata through resources/list, and read selected URI contents with resources/read."],"explanation":"MCP separates server capabilities by control model. Resources are application-controlled contextual data, tools are model-controlled executable functions, and prompts are user-controlled templates. A content catalog belongs in resources because the host or client can decide how to show, filter, search, or attach available context without asking the model to discover the catalog through tool calls. In practice, the server declares the resources capability, the client discovers entries with resources/list , and each entry is identified by a uri . The client can later retrieve the selected item with resources/read , receiving text or binary content depending on the resource. This design keeps discovery lightweight and explicit while preserving Claude's context for the actual coding, refactoring, or documentation work. Tools are still appropriate when Claude needs to take an action, such as creating an issue or running a query. Prompts are useful for reusable workflows, such as a code review template. Neither is the best fit for a host-managed inventory of available reference material. See the MCP overview for the primitive control model at MCP server features and the resource protocol details at MCP resources . MCP resources are the application-controlled primitive for exposing contextual data such as files, schemas, and application-specific information. Listing metadata with resources/list and retrieving selected content with resources/read lets the host manage discovery and context attachment without requiring Claude to call a discovery tool.","domain":2,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q007","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. After a long plan-mode investigation, a developer saves the session ID before making changes. They want to test a minimal patch and a larger refactor from the same analyzed context, but both attempts will touch overlapping files in the same working directory. Which approach best supports independent exploration without confusing conversation history with file state?","options":["Use continue twice from the project directory, and rely on Claude Code to select the baseline automatically.","Resume the saved session ID with the fork option for each attempt, and checkpoint files before edits.","Fork the saved session once, and run both attempts in the same working directory as isolated file branches.","Resume the saved session ID twice without forking, and ask Claude to keep each attempt separate."],"explanation":"Forking is the right tool when the valuable asset is prior conversational context: prompts, tool calls, tool results, and Claude's previous analysis. A fork starts a new session from a copy of an existing session's history, with its own session ID, while the original session remains unchanged. That is ideal for comparing divergent plans that should share the same investigation baseline. The important boundary is that a session is not a filesystem snapshot. If forked sessions edit files in the same working directory, those edits affect the real files and can be seen by other sessions using that directory. When file changes need to be reverted, the documented mechanism is file checkpointing , with the limitation that it tracks supported file-editing tools such as Write , Edit , and NotebookEdit , not arbitrary changes made through shell commands. continue , resume , and fork solve different problems. Use resume when you need a specific prior session ID, use fork with that resumed session when you need an independent conversation branch, and do not rely on natural-language instructions to simulate session isolation. See the official Agent SDK sessions documentation and file checkpointing documentation for the exact session and file-state boundaries. Forking from a saved session creates new conversation histories that start from the same baseline while leaving the original session history unchanged. Because a session is not a filesystem snapshot, file checkpointing is needed if the developer wants a documented way to revert supported file edits.","domain":1,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q008","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. While maintaining the system described above, this team keeps citation validation tests beside the agent code they exercise, including files under packages/search/, packages/synthesis/, and apps/reporting/. The team wants Claude Code to apply the same testing conventions whenever editing these test files, without loading those conventions for unrelated production files. What should the team do?","options":["Create separate CLAUDE.md files in each package directory that repeat the same testing conventions for local files.","Create a Markdown rule file under .claude/rules/ with YAML frontmatter using paths glob patterns for the test file names.","Add the testing conventions to each developer's user-level CLAUDE.md so Claude Code loads them during edits.","Add the testing conventions to the root CLAUDE.md so Claude Code always loads them for the repository."],"explanation":"For conventions that should follow a file type rather than a directory, Claude Code path-specific rules are the right fit. A Markdown file in .claude/rules/ can include YAML frontmatter with the exact paths field, for example patterns that match test files wherever they live in the repository. This design avoids the maintenance burden of copying instructions into multiple directory-level CLAUDE.md files. It also avoids the context bloat of placing test-only guidance in a broadly loaded CLAUDE.md , whether at the project level or in each developer's user-level memory. The mental model is that CLAUDE.md is best for broadly applicable memory, while .claude/rules/ lets a project attach narrower instructions to matching paths. The rule files remain ordinary Markdown project files, and the documented paths frontmatter provides the conditional activation behavior. See Claude Code memory for the documented rule-file structure and path scoping. This uses Claude Code's path-specific rule mechanism for project instructions that should apply only to matching files. Glob patterns such as test file name patterns can match files by type across multiple directories, so the rule is not tied to a single folder layout.","domain":3,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q009","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. A compliance review finds that, in rare pull requests, the system posts an approving review before the required test-result artifact has been attached. The rule is non-negotiable: approval actions must not execute unless the artifact is already present and passing. What change should this team make?","options":["Add a UserPromptSubmit command hook that appends artifact-check instructions to approval requests.","Add a stronger system prompt that instructs Claude to verify artifacts before approvals.","Add a PostToolUse command hook that checks the artifact after approval commands complete.","Add a PreToolUse command hook that denies approval commands when the passing artifact is absent."],"explanation":"When a CI/CD rule is non-negotiable , the enforcement point should be outside the model's voluntary behavior. Claude Code hooks are designed as lifecycle automation points that provide deterministic control, so required actions happen, or forbidden actions are blocked, without relying on the LLM to choose correctly every time. For a rule that must stop an approval before it is posted, PreToolUse is the relevant hook event because it fires before tool execution and can block the call. A command hook can inspect the event-specific JSON it receives on stdin , check whether the passing test artifact exists, and deny the action with feedback when the condition is not met. PostToolUse is too late for this case because it fires after a successful tool call. A stronger prompt or a UserPromptSubmit hook that appends instructions may improve behavior, but those approaches still rely on model compliance rather than blocking the specific tool call. See the official Claude Code documentation on Hooks guide and Hooks . A PreToolUse hook runs before the tool call executes, so it is the right enforcement point for blocking an approval action. Using a non-LLM command hook makes the rule deterministic rather than dependent on whether Claude chooses to follow an instruction.","domain":1,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q010","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. After three months, the system described above assigns a high confidence score to most structured review findings and generated test recommendations. Manual review capacity is being reduced, but the team still needs to measure production error rates and catch rare failure modes in areas like migrations, security-sensitive changes, and language-specific conventions. What monitoring approach best fits this goal?","options":["Sample only low-confidence findings within queues for human review, then treat high-confidence accepted pull requests as error-free cases.","Sample high-confidence findings uniformly across all repositories, then publish one aggregate error rate for the whole pipeline.","Increase the confidence cutoff for findings before merging, then discontinue review for outputs above the new cutoff.","Sample high-confidence findings within strata for change type, component, language, and finding category, then label errors and novel patterns separately."],"explanation":"For a CI review system, high confidence should not mean no measurement . It should mean the output is eligible for lighter review, while a designed sample of those outputs is still checked so the team can estimate real error rates and detect unexpected failures. Stratified random sampling is the right pattern when errors may vary by segment. Sampling within strata such as change type, component, language, and finding category gives visibility into segments that might be rare overall but operationally important. This avoids the common trap of relying on aggregate accuracy, where a strong overall number can hide poor performance on a small but high-impact class of pull requests. Anthropic’s evaluation guidance emphasizes defining task-specific, measurable success criteria and designing evaluations that mirror real-world task distributions, including edge cases. That maps directly to ongoing production monitoring: choose strata that reflect meaningful differences in the CI workload, label sampled outputs, track per-stratum error rates, and record novel patterns so prompts, rubrics, or review routing can be improved. See Define success criteria . This preserves visibility into high-confidence outputs while focusing limited reviewer capacity on meaningful segments of the production distribution. Stratifying by factors that affect review quality helps reveal pockets of poor performance that a single overall metric could hide.","domain":5,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q011","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. During evaluation, the final reports are well written but sometimes include unsupported claims and miss contradictions between sections. The current design asks the report generator to review its own draft before publishing. What architecture change would most effectively improve review reliability?","options":["Delegate verification to listed subagents without passing source excerpts, relying on shared session context to expose prior findings.","Have the synthesis agent merge all subagent outputs once, then instruct it to mark unsupported claims before publishing.","Ask the report generator to perform an extended self-review immediately after drafting, using its existing conversation history.","Run independent reviewer agents on section-specific claim-source bundles, then run a separate integration pass for conflicts and coverage gaps."],"explanation":"For high-stakes synthesis, a stronger pattern is to separate generation from review . The report generator's self-review can catch obvious formatting problems, but it is less likely to challenge the assumptions embedded in its own draft. Independent reviewer agents provide a fresh context, and multi-pass review keeps each pass focused: first verify local claim-source alignment, then check cross-section consistency and coverage. With Claude subagents, the important reliability property is isolated conversation context : a subagent receives its own prompt and task context rather than automatically seeing the coordinator's prior findings. Tool access is governed by configuration, so it may be inherited or restricted depending on how the subagent is defined, and MCP access can be inherited, referenced, or scoped depending on setup. That means the coordinator must explicitly pass the relevant claims, excerpts, source URLs, document names, and other metadata into each reviewer task. See Subagents and Agent SDK overview . The same design principle applies when using tool-based orchestration rather than subagents. Claude can emit multiple tool calls in one response, but your application is responsible for executing them and returning matched results correctly. Parallel review is valuable when the checks are independent, while a later integration pass is better for contradictions and gaps that only appear after comparing sections. See Parallel tool use . Independent review reduces the chance that the same generation context will reinforce its own mistakes. Splitting review into section-level verification and a later integration pass helps preserve focus while still catching cross-section issues.","domain":4,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q012","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. The coordinator delegates pull request review work to separate subagents for security, test coverage, and maintainability. Each subagent returns useful findings to the coordinator, but the final review summary often omits specific file paths, failing test names, and evidence snippets that the coordinator already received. What change best addresses this reliability issue?","options":["Store the security and test coverage instructions in CLAUDE.md for the next CI pipeline run.","Tell the review synthesis subagent to infer missing details from the coordinator's earlier conversation history.","Run the synthesis subagent before the specialized review subagents to reduce context size.","Pass each prior subagent's complete structured findings directly into the review synthesis subagent's prompt."],"explanation":"In a multi-agent review workflow, the coordinator is responsible for moving information between specialized workers. Because subagents are separate agent instances , normal and named subagents run in fresh isolated conversations and do not automatically receive the parent conversation history or prior tool results. The reliable pattern is to pass prior outputs as structured context in the Agent tool prompt, for example findings grouped by file, severity, evidence excerpt, command output, and recommended fix. The synthesis subagent can also have its own system prompt, tool definitions, and potentially memory such as CLAUDE.md , but those do not replace the need to provide the run-specific findings it must synthesize. Asking the synthesis subagent to infer from another agent's history is an anti-pattern for orchestration reliability. CLAUDE.md is useful for stable project guidance, but it is not a substitute for passing pull-request-specific findings into the active handoff. Learn more about subagent isolation and prompt handoffs in the Agent SDK subagents documentation. Subagents are separate agent instances, so the synthesis subagent should be given the information it needs explicitly. Passing structured findings, such as file paths, test names, evidence, and severity, lets the synthesis step ground its final review in the actual outputs from prior agents.","domain":1,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q013","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. This team wants an on-demand Claude Code workflow named /case-review for support engineers. The workflow produces lengthy exploratory notes, should run outside the main conversation context, should let Claude read and search project files without repeated permission prompts, and should show the expected case ID when developers invoke it. Which setup best fits these requirements?","options":["Create .claude/skills/case-review/SKILL.md with context: fork, allowed-tools for Read and Grep, and argument-hint: [case-id].","Create .claude/skills/case-review/SKILL.md with context: main, allowed-tools for Read and Grep, and argument-hint: [case-id].","Create .claude/skills/case-review/SKILL.md with context: fork, allowed-tools as an exclusive allowlist, and argument-hint as a validator.","Create .claude/skills/case-review.md with context: fork, allowed-tools for Read and Grep, and argument-hint: [case-id]."],"explanation":"Claude Code skills package reusable workflows as directories containing a required SKILL.md . The file can include YAML frontmatter between --- markers plus Markdown instructions, and a project skill stored under .claude/skills//SKILL.md can be invoked directly as /skill-name or loaded when relevant. For this workflow, context: fork is the key frontmatter setting because the skill performs exploratory work that could otherwise clutter the main conversation. allowed-tools is appropriate when the goal is to pre-approve tools such as Read and Grep during the skill, but it should not be treated as a security boundary that blocks every unlisted tool. If a team needs to remove tools while a skill is active, the documented mechanism is disallowed-tools , with broader blocking handled through permission deny rules. argument-hint improves usability by showing expected arguments such as [case-id] during autocomplete. It is not a schema, validator, or runtime enforcement mechanism, so any required argument validation must be handled elsewhere in the workflow. Learn more in the official Claude Code documentation for Skills and the Slash Commands and skill frontmatter reference . A Claude Code skill is defined as a directory containing a required SKILL.md file with YAML frontmatter and Markdown instructions. context: fork runs the skill in an isolated subagent context, allowed-tools pre-approves listed tools while the skill is active, and argument-hint displays invocation guidance in autocomplete.","domain":3,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q014","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. A customer support platform uses the Messages API client tool-use loop. During beta testing, policy requires refunds above $500 to be reviewed by a human. In several high-emotion billing disputes, the agent emits a process_refund tool_use request for amounts above that threshold after lookup_order, and the application executes the request. What change would most effectively enforce the workflow requirement?","options":["Force tool_choice to the escalate_to_human tool for every billing dispute involving a refund request.","Set tool_choice to none whenever the customer message includes emotionally charged billing language.","Add an application-side guard that blocks over-threshold process_refund calls and routes the case to escalate_to_human.","Update the system prompt to state that over-threshold refunds must always be sent to escalate_to_human."],"explanation":"For client-executed tools in the Messages API tool-use loop, Claude does not run the backend operation itself. Claude emits a structured tool_use request, then the application decides whether and how to execute the requested operation and sends back a tool_result . That makes the application boundary the right place to enforce business rules that must not be bypassed. A refund threshold is a workflow enforcement problem, not just an instruction-following problem. The model can be guided to escalate, but deterministic control comes from validating the outgoing tool call before execution and refusing or redirecting calls that violate policy. This preserves autonomy for routine cases while requiring human review for high-impact exceptions. tool_choice is useful for controlling whether Claude may use tools, must use some tool, must use a named tool, or cannot use tools. It is not a substitute for policy validation around a client-executed backend action. Learn more about the client tool loop and tool_choice behavior in How tool use works . This enforces the policy at the point where the client-executed tool would actually run, so the model cannot bypass it by emitting a tool_use request. It also preserves the agentic workflow for allowed refunds while deterministically routing high-impact exceptions to human review.","domain":1,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q015","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. During an incident review, this team needs Claude Code to understand where refund escalation decisions are made in a large repository. Previous investigations loaded entire service directories into context before identifying the relevant flow, which made the session noisy and slow. What approach should the team use first?","options":["Use Glob for refund and escalation identifiers, then infer the control flow from the returned matching paths.","Use Read across the service directories, then summarize all files before searching for refund and escalation identifiers.","Use Grep for refund and escalation identifiers, then Read only matching files and follow imports from those entry points.","Use Bash for broad shell searches, then paste full command output before deciding which files to inspect."],"explanation":"For large codebase exploration, the practical pattern is search first, read second . Claude Code's Grep tool is designed to search for patterns in file contents, while Read loads the contents of specific files. Starting with content search lets the agent find likely entry points such as refund handlers, escalation checks, or MCP tool wrappers before spending context on full file contents. This is different from using Glob , which is useful when the goal is to find files by path or name pattern, such as **/*.test.ts . It is also usually better than broad shell output because Claude Code already provides purpose-built tools whose outputs can be scoped to the investigation. Once matching files are found, Read should be used selectively, then imports, callers, and adjacent abstractions can be followed step by step. The mental model is to treat context as a limited working set. Broad reading gives Claude many tokens before it knows what matters, while Grep creates a small map of relevant files and references. The documented tool distinctions are described in Anthropic's Claude Code tools reference . This approach builds understanding incrementally by using Grep to search inside files for relevant identifiers, then using Read on the files that are actually implicated. It preserves context by starting from evidence-based entry points rather than loading broad directory contents upfront.","domain":2,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q016","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. A nightly pull request check for this team invokes Claude Code to inspect changes to refund and escalation logic. In CI, the job prints the prompt and then waits until the runner times out because there is no terminal user to answer follow-up prompts. What change best prevents this failure mode?","options":["Create a shared slash command in .claude/commands/ and invoke that command from the CI job.","Add a CLAUDE.md instruction telling Claude Code not to ask follow-up questions during CI reviews.","Run Claude Code with the -p or --print flag and provide the review prompt non-interactively.","Run Claude Code in plan mode and require the job to approve the generated plan automatically."],"explanation":"Claude Code has an interactive terminal mode for developer sessions and a non-interactive mode for automation. In CI, there is no human sitting at the terminal to answer prompts, approve actions, or continue a conversation, so the invocation should use -p or --print to process a prompt and exit. Configuration files such as CLAUDE.md and project slash commands are still useful in this workflow because they give CI-invoked Claude Code the same standards, review criteria, and reusable instructions as the rest of the team. They solve what Claude should know or do , not how the CLI process should run . Plan mode is similarly a workflow choice for human review of complex changes, not the mechanism that prevents an unattended runner from hanging. For CI design, keep the mental model simple: use -p for unattended execution, add structured output options when downstream systems need machine-readable results, and keep project context in version-controlled configuration. Learn more in the Claude Code CLI reference . The -p or --print flag is designed for non-interactive Claude Code runs, such as CI jobs. It processes the supplied prompt, writes the result, and exits instead of waiting for additional terminal input.","domain":3,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q017","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. This team’s custom /upgrade-plan command parses repository upgrade guides into JSON for downstream automation. Evals show valid JSON, but required fields such as rollback_command and compatibility_note are returned as null when the source expresses them in fenced shell blocks, Markdown checklists, or prose paragraphs. The information is present in the source, and the current prompt already lists the schema and says not to omit required fields. What change would most directly address the extraction failures?","options":["Add 3 to 5 structured examples that map varied guide formats to the expected JSON fields.","Move the schema description into CLAUDE.md so it is always loaded for the command.","Add a stronger instruction that required JSON fields must never be null or empty.","Raise the temperature to encourage broader interpretation of each upgrade guide format."],"explanation":"When extraction fails even though the source contains the information, the prompt often needs to teach Claude the mapping behavior , not merely repeat the target schema. Few-shot or multishot prompting is designed for this kind of consistency problem: the examples show that a rollback command in a fenced shell block, a checklist item, or a prose sentence can all populate the same JSON field. Anthropic recommends making examples relevant , diverse , and structured , with examples wrapped in tags and multiple examples grouped inside . For this command, the best examples would mirror real upgrade guides and include both the source excerpt and the ideal extracted JSON, so Claude learns the decision boundary without being pushed to invent values. See Use examples (multishot prompting) to guide Claude's behavior . Few-shot or multishot examples are well suited when a model must learn how different input patterns correspond to the same output fields. Relevant, diverse, structured examples can demonstrate that fenced commands, checklist items, and prose notes all count as valid evidence for the required fields.","domain":4,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q018","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. This team’s CI review prompt flags possible policy violations and requires each finding to include a policy_section and supporting_quote. A pull request adds retention logic for customer support transcripts, but the only policy excerpt provided to Claude covers application logs and analytics events, with no transcript policy. The validator rejects Claude’s finding because supporting_quote is empty. What should the pipeline do?","options":["Return an insufficient information result and require a supporting quote before posting the finding.","Increase max_tokens and continue the response to complete the missing policy evidence.","Force a strict structured output schema so supporting_quote is always present.","Retry the same review prompt until Claude produces a non-empty supporting_quote field."],"explanation":"Validation-retry loops are useful when Claude’s output is malformed, incomplete, or structurally inconsistent with the expected format. They are not a mechanism for discovering facts that are absent from the source material. When a CI review finding requires evidence, the safer design is to let the system emit insufficient information rather than retry until a claim appears. Anthropic’s hallucination guidance emphasizes allowing Claude to say it does not know, restricting answers to provided documents, and requiring supporting quotes or citations for factual claims. The Reduce hallucinations guidance supports retracting claims that lack source support, which is exactly the right behavior for automated code review findings that could create false positives. Structured outputs help with format reliability, not factual availability. Features such as JSON schemas and strict tool use can make output easier to parse, but they do not prove that a policy passage exists. The Structured outputs documentation describes schema conformance and limitations, while the Handling stop reasons documentation distinguishes cases like max_tokens , where continuing may be appropriate, from situations where the input simply lacks evidence. For review systems, this distinction matters because unsupported findings erode developer trust. If the policy text does not mention customer support transcripts, the pipeline should report that the evidence is unavailable, route the item for human policy clarification if needed, and avoid posting an automated violation. When citations are available, the Citations feature provides auditable source passages and locations, reinforcing the same principle: claims should be tied to evidence that actually exists in the supplied content. This treats the missing supporting quote as evidence that the supplied source does not support the claim. Retries can fix malformed output, but they cannot create a policy passage that was not provided.","domain":4,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q019","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. For documentation produced during this workflow, the team runs an extraction job that converts design docs and pull request descriptions into JSON work items. Reviewers can inspect only about 20% of items. The current job sends any response with stop_reason: end_turn directly into the tracker, and spot checks find bad work items when two source files disagree or a field is inferred weakly from context. What change best improves the review workflow?","options":["Add per-field confidence, ambiguity, contradiction, and evidence-excerpt fields to the JSON schema, then queue flagged records before clean records.","Enable citations on the source documents alongside JSON schema output, then queue records missing citation blocks before other records for reviewers.","Use end_turn as the acceptance signal, retry max_tokens outputs with more tokens, then queue refusals and malformed records for reviewers.","Request logprobs and top_logprobs through the OpenAI SDK layer, then queue low-probability records before well-formed records for reviewers."],"explanation":"For high-stakes extraction workflows, review routing should be driven by application-defined uncertainty signals , not by API mechanics that were designed for other purposes. stop_reason tells the application whether Claude ended normally, requested a tool, hit a token limit, refused, or encountered another stopping condition; it does not say that an extracted field is trustworthy. A practical design is to use a documented JSON schema structured-output approach for a consistent extraction shape, and include fields such as per-field confidence, source excerpt, ambiguity flag, contradiction flag, and missing-evidence flag. Structured output helps downstream systems parse the result reliably, but schema compliance is not the same as factual correctness . Human reviewers should be prioritized toward records where the model marks uncertainty, source documents conflict, evidence is missing, or validation logic detects an inconsistency. Citations are a separate grounding mechanism for document and search-result answers, but they cannot be mixed with structured JSON outputs in the same request. If a workflow needs JSON extraction, use explicit evidence fields inside the schema rather than citation blocks. See Structured outputs , Handling stop reasons , OpenAI SDK compatibility , and Citations . This creates application-defined review signals that map directly to the observed failure modes: weak inference and contradictory sources. A JSON schema can standardize the extraction shape, while reviewer routing should use explicit fields and validation results rather than assuming a successful stop reason means the content is reliable.","domain":5,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q020","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. This team wants Claude Code to work with Jira issues for everyday development tasks, and also needs a release-readiness workflow that combines Jira fields with private service ownership rules and internal deployment checks. What integration approach best balances reuse, maintainability, and team-specific behavior?","options":["Configure a maintained Jira MCP server for Jira operations, and implement a custom MCP server for the release-readiness workflow.","Store Jira REST procedures in CLAUDE.md and drive ticket updates through slash commands that invoke Bash.","Build one custom MCP server that reimplements Jira operations and also handles the release-readiness workflow.","Create project slash commands for each Jira action and rely on Claude Code prompts to choose API calls."],"explanation":"MCP helps avoid the N-by-M integration problem: every AI host should not need to hand-build every service integration from scratch. When a standard service integration already exists, such as Jira issue lookup or ticket updates, using a maintained MCP server usually reduces schema design work, implementation effort, and long-term maintenance. A custom MCP server is still appropriate when the workflow depends on private systems, team-specific policies, or proprietary orchestration that a general-purpose server cannot know about. In this case, release readiness depends on internal ownership rules and deployment checks, so a small custom server can expose that workflow cleanly while leaving ordinary Jira operations to an existing server. Claude Code can connect to MCP servers and use their exposed tools as part of development workflows. Learn more in Anthropic's Claude Code MCP documentation and the Model Context Protocol documentation . MCP is designed to reduce custom integration work by letting clients use servers that already expose standard tools for common services. Standard Jira operations are a strong fit for an existing maintained server, while the private release-readiness workflow justifies a custom server because it encodes team-specific business logic.","domain":2,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q021","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. Your team created a Claude Code skill called `literature-gap-audit`. Its `SKILL.md` tells Claude to search repository prompts, inspect report templates, and brainstorm missing provenance checks before anyone edits the coordinator. When developers invoke it, pages of file-search notes and rejected alternatives remain in the main session, and later report-generation edits become less focused. What should you change in the skill configuration?","options":["Add `context: fork` in `SKILL.md` and keep the audit as explicit task instructions.","Invoke `/fork` for the audit so it inherits the full current conversation history.","Move the audit instructions into `CLAUDE.md` and run `/compact` after the audit finishes.","Set `disable-model-invocation: true` and leave the audit in the main conversation."],"explanation":"Use context: fork when a Claude Code skill performs a side task that would otherwise flood the main conversation with search results, logs, file contents, or exploratory alternatives. In a forked skill, the rendered SKILL.md content drives work in a separate subagent context, and the main session receives the useful result rather than the full discovery trail. This pattern fits task and workflow skills with explicit instructions, such as an audit that searches prompts and report templates before recommending provenance improvements. It is not a good fit for passive reference material, such as API conventions, because the subagent would receive guidelines without a concrete task to perform. CLAUDE.md , /compact , and disable-model-invocation solve different problems. CLAUDE.md is persistent context, /compact compresses an already noisy conversation, and disable-model-invocation controls automatic skill invocation. Also avoid confusing skill frontmatter context: fork with /fork : /fork can isolate a side task while inheriting the full conversation, but it is not a SKILL.md configuration setting. Learn more in the official Claude Code slash commands and skills documentation , the Subagents documentation , and the Claude Code context documentation . This runs the skill in a forked subagent context, so verbose discovery work happens outside the main conversation and only the useful result returns. It is appropriate because the skill contains an actionable audit workflow, not just passive reference guidance.","domain":3,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q022","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. This team uses Claude Code to generate release notes and API documentation from pull requests. Reviewers can check only a small fraction of generated fields before publishing, and pilots show accuracy differs by field, with endpoint names usually correct and breaking-change labels often wrong. What change best routes human review to the items most likely to benefit from it?","options":["Treat successful stop reasons as high confidence, skip review for those outputs, and review only truncated responses.","Emit per-field confidence values in structured output, calibrate thresholds on labeled examples, and route low-confidence fields to reviewers.","Compute one document-level confidence score, review documents below a threshold, and publish all fields in approved documents.","Use aggregate accuracy from the pilot, reduce review when it exceeds target, and sample remaining outputs uniformly."],"explanation":"For production extraction and documentation workflows, the useful unit of review is often the field , not the whole response. A generated release note might have a correct endpoint name, an uncertain migration instruction, and an incorrect breaking-change label. Asking Claude to emit per-field confidence values in a structured format gives downstream systems something to route on, but those values should be treated as model outputs that require calibration, not as native API probabilities. Calibration requires a labeled validation set that reflects the real workflow, including edge cases and fields with different risk profiles. By comparing predicted confidence with observed correctness for each field, the team can set review thresholds that focus limited reviewer capacity where it adds the most value. This is also why aggregate accuracy is insufficient, since strong performance on easy fields can hide poor performance on fields that matter most. Structured output can help make the confidence fields and extracted values parseable, but it does not guarantee factual correctness or calibrated confidence. Robust implementations should also check reliability metadata such as stop_reason , especially for refusals or truncation, before trusting parsed output. See Structured outputs , Define success criteria , and Handling stop reasons . This targets reviewer attention at the field level rather than treating every generated document as equally risky. The labeled validation set is what turns raw model-provided confidence values into operational thresholds that reflect observed accuracy for each field.","domain":5,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q023","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. The team’s /release-note workflow rewrites raw commit messages into customer-facing changelog entries. Reviewers notice inconsistent transformations: internal ticket IDs sometimes remain, breaking changes are sometimes softened into vague language, and terse bug-fix commits are sometimes expanded with unsupported details. What change would most directly improve consistency for this workflow?","options":["Run the workflow in plan mode so Claude outlines the changelog strategy before editing text.","Move the release-note instructions into project settings so the workflow uses shared team configuration.","Add a PreToolUse hook that blocks writes when release notes contain internal ticket IDs.","Add three representative before-and-after examples, including edge cases, in a clearly tagged examples section."],"explanation":"When Claude produces inconsistent transformations from the same kind of input, the most effective improvement is usually to show the desired mapping directly. A few well-chosen input/output examples demonstrate judgment that prose often leaves ambiguous: what to remove, what to preserve, how much to rewrite, and how to handle edge cases. Anthropic’s prompting guidance recommends using relevant, diverse, structured examples for few-shot or multishot prompting, and wrapping them in tags such as or so Claude can distinguish examples from task instructions. For a Claude Code workflow, those examples could live in the relevant skill or command instructions, or in supporting files if the examples grow large. Settings and hooks solve different problems. settings.json configures Claude Code behavior such as permissions and tools, while hooks can deterministically block or react to tool use. Those mechanisms are valuable for enforcement, but they do not replace examples when the issue is an ambiguous transformation standard. See Multishot prompting , Claude Code memory , Claude Code slash commands , and Claude Code settings . Concrete input/output examples teach the desired transformation boundary more reliably than prose alone. Including edge cases such as ticket IDs, breaking changes, and terse bug fixes shows Claude how to handle the exact ambiguity causing inconsistent output.","domain":3,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q024","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. During pilot testing, the refund recommendation step now always produces parseable structured data with required fields present and numeric types correct. The remaining bad cases involve merchandise credits placed in the shipping_refund field or itemized adjustments that do not match the requested refund total. What change should this team make next?","options":["Add downstream business-rule validation before refund execution and feed specific failures back for correction.","Rely on strict tool inputs to guarantee the refund recommendation is operationally correct.","Switch to plain JSON instructions and remove tool schemas from the extraction step.","Make every refund field required in the schema and reject nullable values during extraction."],"explanation":"Schema-based structured output is best understood as controlling shape , not automatically proving meaning . Anthropic documents structured outputs as mechanisms for valid JSON matching a schema, and strict tool use as a way to constrain tool names and tool inputs using JSON Schema and grammar-constrained sampling. Those mechanisms are valuable because they reduce failures like malformed JSON, omitted required fields, and wrong data types. They do not, by themselves, know that a shipping refund was placed in a merchandise field, or that an itemized refund total should match a calculated amount. In a support workflow with financial consequences, the right pattern is to keep the structured schema and add downstream validation for business invariants, then send specific validation failures back for correction or route the case appropriately. See Anthropic's Structured outputs documentation for the distinction between JSON schema conformance and output control, and Strict tool use for how strict tool inputs constrain tool calls. This addresses failures that are valid at the schema level but wrong in business meaning. Tool schemas can enforce JSON shape, required fields, and types, but code must still check domain rules such as totals, policy limits, and field semantics.","domain":4,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q025","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. A refund operation sometimes fails because the order is outside the return window, but the agent treats the MCP response as a successful refund and tells the customer it was completed. What change should this team make to communicate the tool failure correctly?","options":["Return a normal tool result containing an `error` field and ask the agent to infer failure from the content.","Return a CallToolResult with `isError: false` and include structured failure details in the result content.","Raise a protocol-level server exception and include structured failure details in the exception message.","Return a CallToolResult with `isError: true` and include structured failure details in the result content."],"explanation":"In MCP, a tool can be invoked successfully at the protocol level while still failing at the application or business-logic level. The right pattern is to return a CallToolResult that marks the result with isError: true , then put useful recovery context in the result content. This distinction matters for a support agent because failures like policy violations, invalid dates, missing orders, or permission denials are not all the same. A structured tool-error result lets the agent reason about the next action, such as explaining a business rule to the customer, correcting an input, retrying a transient operation, or escalating to a human. The spelling and value are both important: MCP uses camelCase isError on CallToolResult , and it must be set to true for a tool-result error. A successful result that merely contains failure text or an error field may look reasonable to a developer, but it does not provide the documented MCP error signal. See the official MCP specification for tool results and error handling at MCP Tools . This uses the MCP tool error pattern directly. The camelCase `isError` flag on `CallToolResult` tells the MCP client and agent that the tool call itself completed but produced an application-level failure.","domain":2,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q026","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. The team keeps refund and escalation policy changes in GitHub and requests managed Claude Code Review only during scheduled review batches. A reviewer comments `@claude review once`, fixes are pushed, and then a second `@claude review once` repeats several resolved findings as new inline comments. They want the next manual re-review to preserve reviewer trust while still surfacing any unresolved issue from the first review. What should they change?","options":["Add `REVIEW.md` guidance that uses prior findings during re-reviews and reports only new or still-unresolved issues.","Keep review instructions unchanged and have maintainers close any duplicate inline threads after each re-review.","Rerun the review from the GitHub Checks tab so Claude Code refreshes comments against the new commit.","Change the manual command to `@claude review` so the PR subscribes to push-triggered reviews for the remaining work."],"explanation":"Repeated review runs need more than access to the current diff. The review should be given a clear comparison task: consider the prior findings, then report only issues that are new or still unaddressed . In managed Claude Code Review, the documented place for review-specific guidance is REVIEW.md , which is injected into the review pipeline as review guidance. The trigger mechanism and the review behavior are separate concerns. @claude review once starts a single manual review, while @claude review starts a review and subscribes the PR to future push-triggered reviews. Switching commands can change when reviews happen, but it does not define how to deduplicate findings across re-runs. Also avoid relying on the GitHub Checks Re-run button for this feature, since the documentation states that it does not retrigger managed Code Review. The practical pattern is to configure the review instructions so re-runs preserve signal quality, then invoke reviews using the documented command flow. See Claude Code Review . `REVIEW.md` is the documented configuration point for review-only guidance in Claude Code Review. This directly addresses duplicate comments by shaping the review behavior rather than relying on trigger mechanics or manual cleanup.","domain":3,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q027","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. After several weeks, developers say the automated reviewer is noisy. Examples show comments about variable names, preferred assertion styles, and missing tests mixed with a few real null dereferences and authorization bypasses. The current prompt says, \"Report only issues you are highly confident about.\" What prompt change would most directly improve review usefulness?","options":["Define testable report and skip rules that focus comments on production bugs, security flaws, broken edge cases, and subtle regressions.","Keep the broad review scope and require each inline comment to include a numerical confidence score and short rationale.","Expand the checklist to include naming, formatting, test coverage, and architecture preferences so comments feel more comprehensive.","Move every automated finding into a single nonblocking summary comment so developers can scan the noise separately."],"explanation":"The failure mode is not that the reviewer lacks confidence metadata, it is that the prompt has not drawn a clear boundary between actionable correctness findings and low-value preference comments. \"High confidence\" is still subjective: a model can be confident that a variable name differs from a local style, even when that comment is not useful for a pull request review. Claude Code Code Review is documented as analyzing GitHub pull requests and posting severity-tagged inline findings for issues such as logic errors, security vulnerabilities, broken edge cases, and subtle regressions. The documentation also notes that its default calibration is for correctness, namely bugs that would break production, not formatting preferences or missing test coverage. See Claude Code Code Review . For CI review prompts, make the decision boundary concrete: report issues when the claimed or implemented behavior can break production, create a security risk, mishandle an edge case, or introduce a regression. Skip comments that are only style, naming, formatting, or local preference unless the team has explicitly made them part of the review objective. This reduces false positives and helps developers keep trusting the comments that matter. This directly addresses the category boundary causing the noise. It tells the reviewer which findings are worth surfacing and which findings to skip, rather than asking it to estimate confidence on subjective issues.","domain":4,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q028","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. An online retailer's support automation team has successful MCP tool calls returning values in inconsistent formats: one response uses Unix timestamps and numeric status codes, while another uses ISO 8601 dates and string status values. Claude sometimes misinterprets these fields when deciding whether an order is eligible for refund. What should this team implement to give the agent a canonical representation before it reasons over successful tool results?","options":["Add a PostToolUse hook that returns hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext with mappings for the inconsistent fields.","Add a PostToolUse hook that reads tool_response and returns hookSpecificOutput.updatedToolOutput with normalized fields.","Add a PostToolUse hook that returns decision: \"block\" when a tool response uses noncanonical fields.","Add a PreToolUse hook that blocks calls whenever the selected tool may return noncanonical fields."],"explanation":"For successful tool calls, a PostToolUse hook is the right interception point for normalizing returned data before Claude reasons over it. The hook input includes tool-dependent fields such as tool_input and tool_response , so the normalization logic must understand each MCP tool's output shape rather than assuming one fixed schema. The important distinction is replacement versus annotation. hookSpecificOutput.updatedToolOutput replaces the tool output before it is sent to Claude, while additionalContext only appends information alongside the original result and decision: \"block\" does not turn the result into a canonical form. Because MCP output is passed through without schema validation by the hook system, the hook should preserve the structure the downstream agent expects while converting values such as timestamps, dates, and status codes. Also remember the timing tradeoff: PostToolUse happens after the tool has already succeeded, so it cannot prevent side effects or change what the backend call did. Use PreToolUse when you need to validate or block a call before execution. See the official hook behavior in Claude Code hooks and the current output replacement guidance in Agent SDK hooks . PostToolUse runs after a successful tool call, which is the right point to transform completed MCP tool results before Claude processes them. The documented updatedToolOutput field replaces the tool output that Claude sees, allowing the hook to canonicalize dates and status values.","domain":1,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q029","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. The repository's main instruction file has grown to more than 400 lines and mixes review tone, test conventions, API design rules, and deployment checklist guidance. CI runs show that Claude Code is applying deployment guidance to routine unit test reviews and sometimes missing the test-specific criteria developers care about. What change should this team make to reduce irrelevant context while keeping shared review standards available?","options":["Create separate CLAUDE.md files in every package directory and duplicate the relevant test, API, and deployment guidance there.","Move all topic guidance into .claude/rules/ files without paths frontmatter and rely on the directory to lazy-load them.","Split the large instruction file into imported Markdown files and reference each one from the root CLAUDE.md with @path imports.","Move topic-specific guidance into Markdown files under .claude/rules/ and add paths frontmatter for rules that apply only to matching files."],"explanation":"For large Claude Code instruction sets, .claude/rules/ is the right mechanism for modular, topic-specific project guidance. Each rule is a Markdown file, such as testing.md or api-design.md , and rules can include YAML paths frontmatter so they apply only when Claude works with files matching glob patterns. The important distinction is between organization and context reduction . @path imports can make a large CLAUDE.md easier for humans to maintain, but imported files are still expanded into context at launch. Path-scoped rules are different because they are loaded only for matching work, which reduces irrelevant guidance during CI review tasks. Rules that omit paths are still useful for always-applicable standards, but they are unconditional. If the goal is to keep deployment guidance out of routine unit test reviews, the deployment rule needs appropriate path scoping rather than merely being moved into the rules directory. See the official Claude Code memory documentation for project rules, path-specific rules, imports, and /memory inspection details at Claude Code Memory . This uses the documented modular rules mechanism for larger projects and allows rules to load only when Claude works with matching file patterns. Keeping each rule topic-focused reduces context noise while preserving shared standards through version-controlled project files.","domain":3,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q030","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. A customer support engineering team is building a resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK with MCP tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). During testing, the agent trace shows Claude requesting lookup_order through MCP, but no order lookup result is observed and the session ends without a final customer response. What implementation change should this team make?","options":["Parse the assistant's natural-language text for completion phrases, then end the workflow when it says the issue is resolved.","Run all support MCP tools before starting the agent, then provide their outputs for Claude to summarize in one turn.","Add a system instruction that tells Claude to execute each MCP backend operation itself before producing any customer-facing response.","Run the SDK-driven agent loop so each MCP tool invocation is dispatched, results are returned, and processing continues until a final response."],"explanation":"Tool use is a contract between Claude and the surrounding runtime. In an Agent SDK system with MCP tools, Claude can decide that it needs a tool and emit a structured tool invocation, but the model itself does not perform backend operations such as customer or order lookups. The practical requirement is that the agent runtime or application must keep the agentic loop alive: dispatch requested MCP tool calls to the connected tool server or runtime, return the results as observations, and let Claude continue reasoning from those observations until it produces a final customer-facing response. If the host process stops after the tool request, the transcript will show a valid request but no completed action or final answer. This design lets Claude choose actions adaptively while preserving a clear execution boundary around external systems. Prompt-only fixes and text parsing confuse guidance with control, while pre-running every tool turns an adaptive agent into a fixed workflow with unnecessary calls. See Anthropic's How tool use works documentation for the underlying tool-use contract and execution responsibilities. Claude can request tool use, but the model does not execute backend operations by itself. The agent runtime or surrounding application must keep processing tool invocations and returned observations until Claude produces a final response.","domain":1,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q031","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. Recent CI runs show that on pull requests touching 20 to 40 files, the review often reports superficial style issues while missing problems that depend on interactions between files. The team wants a more reliable review design without turning the review into an open-ended investigation. What change should they make?","options":["Run independent parallel reviewers for file groups and publish their comments without a synthesis step.","Let Claude dynamically choose its own review path and tools for each pull request.","Send the entire diff to one reviewer prompt and raise the severity threshold for comments.","Run a prompt chain that reviews files in focused passes, then runs a separate integration pass over the collected findings."],"explanation":"For predictable CI review tasks, a prompt chaining workflow is a strong fit because the desired process is known before the run starts: inspect focused units of work, preserve the findings, then perform a broader consistency check. This is different from asking an agent to dynamically decide the review strategy for every pull request. The important tradeoff is focus versus coverage. A single large review prompt has broad context, but it can spread attention thinly across many files. Per-file review stages improve local precision, while a later cross-file integration pass gives Claude a dedicated opportunity to reason about API contracts, shared types, data flow, and behavioral consistency across the change set. Anthropic distinguishes workflows, which orchestrate LLMs and tools through predefined code paths, from agents, which dynamically direct their own process and tool use. See Building effective agents for the workflow versus agent distinction and the role of prompt chaining patterns. This applies a predefined workflow that reduces attention dilution during large reviews. The per-file passes improve local issue detection, while the integration pass is where cross-file concerns can be checked deliberately.","domain":1,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q032","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. A university library's multi-agent research system routes tasks to MCP-backed web search and document analysis subagents. During evaluation, the coordinator often sends literature-review tasks to the web search subagent when the user asked for analysis of uploaded PDFs, and sends web-currentness tasks to the document analysis subagent. The tool implementations work correctly once called. Which tool-definition change should this team make first to improve selection reliability?","options":["Add request examples inside JSON Schema field descriptions for web queries, PDF analysis, and citation searches.","Rewrite each tool definition with detailed descriptions covering use cases, non-use cases, inputs, returned data, and caveats.","Force each research turn through the model API to call one available tool before returning results to the coordinator.","Rename each tool with shorter service-prefixed names while leaving the current descriptions and schemas unchanged."],"explanation":"When several tools have similar capabilities, tool descriptions are the highest-leverage place to improve selection reliability. Claude decides whether and which tool to call from the user request plus the tool definition, and Anthropic identifies detailed descriptions as a major factor in tool performance. A good description is not just a label. It should explain what the tool does, when it should and should not be used, what inputs mean, what output is returned, and any limitations or edge cases. That gives the model decision boundaries, which is exactly what overlapping research tools need. Forcing tool use can be useful when the problem is refusal to call any tool, but it does not solve incorrect selection among similar tools. Examples placed in supported descriptive fields may help with input formatting, and namespacing may help organize large catalogs, but neither replaces clear descriptions. See Anthropic's guidance on defining tools and the overview of Claude tool use . Claude selects tools from the conversation and tool definition, with the description being especially important for when to use a tool. Making the descriptions explicit about when to use and not use each tool directly addresses ambiguity among similar capabilities.","domain":2,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q033","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. The team wants a reusable workflow for adding comprehensive tests to unfamiliar legacy modules. The work usually begins with reading the module, but the next steps vary depending on discovered dependencies, existing test patterns, and whether code seams exist. They care more about producing useful coverage than minimizing latency. What orchestration choice best fits?","options":["Use dynamic adaptive decomposition so Claude inspects the module, creates subtasks from findings, and synthesizes the test plan.","Use fixed prompt chaining so Claude always analyzes files, writes tests, runs tests, and fixes failures in that order.","Use one broad slash command so Claude receives all files and requirements in a single unconstrained request.","Use direct execution so Claude immediately writes tests, then handles any missing context after failures appear."],"explanation":"Anthropic distinguishes workflows , which follow predefined code paths, from more agentic patterns where the model dynamically directs its own process and tool usage. Prompt chaining is a fixed workflow pattern: one step feeds the next, and it is strongest when the task naturally breaks into stable, known subtasks. Adding tests to unfamiliar legacy modules is different. The first inspection can reveal hidden dependencies, missing test seams, or unusual project conventions, so the workflow benefits from dynamic adaptive decomposition . The relevant documented pattern is orchestrator-workers , where a central LLM breaks down the task, assigns or performs subtasks, and synthesizes the result based on what it learns. This choice carries a tradeoff. Adaptive orchestration usually costs more latency and tokens than a fixed chain, but that tradeoff is justified when intermediate findings determine the next useful action. For a predictable checklist, use prompt chaining; for open-ended investigation, use adaptive decomposition. See Anthropic's guidance in Building effective agents . This is the best fit because the useful subtasks are not known until Claude inspects the codebase. An adaptive orchestrator-workers style lets a central process decide what to investigate next based on intermediate findings, which matches open-ended legacy test work.","domain":1,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q034","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. A customer support resolution agent's production logs show that process_refund sometimes returns a successful-looking tool result with content \"Refund blocked by policy\" when an order is outside the refund window. Claude then retries the same refund call with slightly different wording instead of explaining the policy outcome or escalating when appropriate. What change would best improve the agent's recovery behavior?","options":["Return a CallToolResult with isError: true and place custom errorCategory, isRetryable, and policy text in structuredContent.","Update the process_refund description to warn Claude not to request refunds outside the configured refund window.","Raise a JSON-RPC protocol error from tools/call whenever the refund request violates a configured business policy.","Return a normal CallToolResult with a longer refund policy explanation and suggested next steps in the content blocks."],"explanation":"For customer support tools, the important distinction is between a successful business outcome , a retryable operational failure , and a non-retryable policy denial . If a backend returns a policy denial as ordinary text in a successful-looking result, Claude may interpret the result as ambiguous and try again. Marking the tool result with isError: true gives the agent the documented MCP signal that the tool execution failed, while custom metadata such as errorCategory and isRetryable can provide the recovery policy your application wants. MCP tool calls are made through the tools/call method with a tool name and optional arguments . A CallToolResult returns content blocks and can also include structured content when the tool provides machine-readable output. Fields such as errorCategory and isRetryable are useful design conventions, but they are not the standard MCP error marker, so they should be treated as custom structured output. The design tradeoff is that structured errors require more implementation discipline, but they make agent behavior more predictable under failure. In a refund workflow, this helps prevent wasteful retry loops and supports safer resolution behavior. See the MCP tool and schema documentation at MCP Tools and MCP Schema . This uses the official MCP error indicator to tell the agent the tool execution did not complete successfully. The category, retryability hint, and user-facing policy message are custom metadata, so they belong in structuredContent or another agreed output schema rather than being treated as standard MCP fields.","domain":2,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q035","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. Your /review command posts PR feedback in four categories: correctness, security, performance, and style. Evaluation data shows security findings are usually accepted, but style comments are often dismissed as local-preference nitpicks. Developers have begun ignoring the whole review report, including security items. What change should you make first?","options":["Raise the severity of all findings, then instruct Claude to be more conservative across every review category.","Track precision and recall by category, then tighten or disable the noisy style criteria using flagged and non-flagged examples.","Track only the overall accepted-finding rate, then revise the prompt when the aggregate metric drops below target.","Move security findings to the top of every report, then label them as mandatory review items for developers."],"explanation":"For review automation, each finding category behaves like a classification problem: Claude is deciding whether a piece of code should be flagged or not. A high false positive rate in one visible category, such as style, can teach developers that the entire review report is noisy, so they may also ignore accurate security or correctness findings. The practical fix is to evaluate and tune at the level where the failure occurs. Track precision and recall by category, inspect the dismissed findings, and refine the category with explicit criteria plus examples of what should and should not be flagged. This mirrors Anthropic's guidance to build classification examples, include edge cases, and monitor precision and recall rather than relying on overall accuracy alone. Moving important findings higher in the report or making labels more forceful can help presentation, but it does not repair the underlying trust problem. The more reliable strategy is to reduce noisy categories before they contaminate confidence in the accurate ones. See Anthropic's Content moderation guide for the classification framing, false positive pitfalls, and production monitoring guidance. This directly addresses the trust failure by identifying the category with excessive false positives and refining its decision boundary. Tracking precision and recall by category avoids hiding a low-trust category behind stronger performance elsewhere.","domain":4,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q036","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. Benchmark runs on broad topics are slow and produce uneven coverage. Logs show the coordinator asks one specialist to research every dimension, passes a compressed prose summary to the next specialist, and only then asks for the final report. The relevant dimensions are known at the start and do not depend on one another. What design change best improves latency and coverage while preserving a coherent final report?","options":["Have the coordinator disable parallel tool use, process each dimension in separate turns, append every raw result, and synthesize after the last subtask.","Have the coordinator split the request into dimension-specific subtasks, pass shared constraints to each, invoke independent specialists together, and synthesize their findings.","Have the coordinator keep one broad subtask, increase the search specialist's source budget, pass its summary downstream, and request a completeness critique.","Have each specialist hand off unfinished dimensions to another specialist, merge their private context, and return a consolidated answer to the coordinator."],"explanation":"For independent research dimensions, the strongest pattern is to split, run, collect, then synthesize . The coordinator should create distinct subtasks, provide each specialist with the shared scope, constraints, and citation requirements, then combine their structured findings into one coherent report. Claude can emit several tool_use blocks in a single assistant turn, and the application decides whether those client-side calls run concurrently, sequentially, or with a mixed strategy. The official guidance on parallel tool use notes that independent, read-only calls are usually good candidates for parallel execution, while work with side effects or ordering dependencies should be handled more carefully. The practical boundary is that parallelism is not a substitute for context design. Each specialist needs the shared instructions and enough source or metadata context to do its work, and the coordinator should collect one tool_result for each tool_use before moving to synthesis. The result formatting requirements are described in handle tool calls , including matching tool_use_id values and returning the results in the next user message. The tradeoff is fan-out cost and complexity. The orchestration guidance in mid-conversation effort example describes this pattern as useful for separable subtasks, but it also cautions that spawning many workstreams can multiply token usage. Use it when the subtasks are genuinely independent and the final synthesis benefits from broader, more balanced coverage. This uses parallel decomposition for independent workstreams while keeping a single coordinator responsible for final synthesis. Passing shared constraints to each specialist preserves the common context needed for consistent scope, citation expectations, and report structure.","domain":1,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q037","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. During a billing dispute flow, Claude requests get_customer and lookup_order in the same assistant turn. The get_customer call succeeds, but lookup_order intermittently times out. Production logs show two attempted fixes: one wrapper returns an empty order list as a successful lookup, while another aborts the entire conversation whenever lookup_order fails. What change best addresses the reliability issue?","options":["Return tool results for completed calls only, omit lookup_order after its timeout, and ask Claude to proceed.","Return tool results for both calls, discard get_customer after lookup_order fails, and end the support workflow.","Return tool results for both calls, replace lookup_order with an empty order list, and continue as a successful lookup.","Return tool results for both calls, flag lookup_order with is_error: true, and continue from partial context."],"explanation":"Tool failures should be treated as part of the agent's observed state , not hidden or converted into misleading business facts. When Claude emits tool_use , the application is expected to execute the requested client tools and return matching tool_result blocks, using the same tool_use_id so Claude can connect each result to the right call. The reliable pattern is to return all available results and mark failed executions with is_error: true . This lets the agent distinguish three materially different situations: the customer has no orders, the order service failed, or the order service returned partial information. Those distinctions matter in support workflows because they drive different next actions, such as retrying, asking a clarifying question, or escalating to a human. Silently returning an empty success is an anti-pattern because it changes an operational failure into false domain evidence. Terminating the whole workflow on one failure is also an anti-pattern because it throws away useful context from successful calls and prevents graceful degradation. The tool-use protocol is designed so the model can continue reasoning from accurate observations, while actual API request failures remain separate from successful Messages API responses and their stop_reason control flow. See Anthropic's guidance on Handle tool calls for tool-result formatting and is_error , and Handling stop reasons for how successful responses require control-flow decisions rather than a single success or failure branch. This preserves the required tool-result protocol while giving Claude accurate information about the failed operation. The agent can use the successful customer data, reason about retrying or escalating, and avoid incorrectly treating a timeout as proof that no orders exist.","domain":5,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q038","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. Your team’s Claude Code skill/custom command, invoked as /task, is meant to decide whether to investigate, plan, or modify code. In evals, ambiguous requests such as “clean this up,” “look into this error,” and “can you improve this module?” produce inconsistent behavior. Detailed written criteria have not stabilized the boundary. What should you change next?","options":["Add a large collection of untagged past transcripts so Claude can infer the team’s preferred behavior from volume.","Add 2-4 tagged examples showing similar ambiguous requests, the selected response path, and concise reasoning against plausible alternatives.","Add a longer checklist that enumerates every phrase that should trigger investigation, planning, or code modification.","Add a stronger instruction that direct file edits must be avoided whenever the developer’s request could imply planning."],"explanation":"When a prompt fails on ambiguous decision boundaries , targeted examples usually outperform more abstract instruction text. The useful pattern is not merely showing the final answer, but showing why one action was chosen over another plausible action, such as planning instead of editing, or investigating instead of proposing a refactor. For a Claude Code skill or existing custom command invoked as a slash command, the examples should mirror real developer requests and be clearly separated from the live input. Anthropic recommends examples that are relevant , diverse , and structured , with and individual tags for clear delimiting. This helps Claude distinguish reusable behavioral guidance from the user’s current request. The distractors all miss a key tradeoff. A blanket “avoid edits” rule improves safety at the cost of overblocking useful work, a phrase checklist is brittle, and unstructured transcripts can introduce accidental patterns. A compact set of targeted examples gives Claude a clearer model of the team’s intended judgment without turning the command into an inflexible decision tree. See Anthropic’s guidance on examples, explicit instructions, and XML prompt structure in Claude prompting best practices . Few-shot or multishot examples are well suited when written criteria do not consistently communicate a judgment boundary. Showing the selected action and the reasoning against plausible alternatives teaches Claude the pattern for resolving similar ambiguous requests.","domain":4,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q039","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. Recent evaluations show that the final report sometimes treats the same source as both confirming and contradicting a claim, and reviewers find that unsupported claims in long source bundles are missed. The team also wants review checkpoints it can log and score before the report is generated. What should the team do?","options":["Launch several identical reviewers on the full source bundle and accept findings that appear most often.","Add confidence scores to every cited claim and route only low-confidence claims to another reviewer.","Send the complete source bundle to one reviewer and require a longer checklist before report generation.","Run an explicit prompt chain with source-level review passes, then run a separate integration pass over the reviewed findings."],"explanation":"The stronger design is to treat this as explicit prompt chaining : use separate API calls for focused source-level review, then pass the resulting structured findings into a cross-source integration review. Anthropic documents prompt chaining as a way to break a task into sequential calls when you need visible intermediate outputs, evaluation points, or a fixed pipeline structure. This pattern is useful because a large context window does not make every large review reliable. Anthropic’s context window guidance describes context rot , where accuracy and recall can degrade as token count grows. Smaller local passes reduce competing attention demands, while the integration pass can focus specifically on consistency, conflict reconciliation, and claim-source alignment. Adding a longer checklist, duplicating the same overloaded review, or relying on confidence scores may improve some cases, but none of them changes the underlying shape of the task. The tradeoff of explicit chaining is extra orchestration and additional calls, but in return the team gets inspectable review artifacts and a clearer separation between local evidence verification and global synthesis. Learn more in Anthropic’s documentation on Chain prompts and Context windows . This approach breaks the review into smaller, sequential steps so each pass can focus on a limited set of evidence. The later integration pass can reconcile conflicts across sources while preserving intermediate artifacts for logging and evaluation.","domain":4,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q040","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. During a long research run, later synthesis turns sometimes ignore findings that the web search and document analysis subagents returned earlier. Logging shows later Messages API calls contain only the current coordinator instruction and the newest subagent output. What change best addresses the coherence problem?","options":["Persist and resend the relevant messages array, including earlier assistant outputs, tool results, and the current user turn.","Move prior findings into the system prompt once and omit the historical messages from later requests.","Enable automatic prompt caching so earlier subagent findings are remembered without being included in later requests.","Store a coordinator session identifier and send only the newest subagent result with each follow-up request."],"explanation":"The practical mental model for the Messages API is that every call is independent. Claude receives the system content, the current messages array, tools, and other request content for that call, then returns a new assistant message. If a prior subagent result is not present in the current request, Claude cannot rely on it as conversational context. For a multi-agent research system, the coordinator must act as the state manager. It should persist the conversation state and assemble each subsequent request with the relevant prior assistant messages, tool results, subagent findings, and the current user turn. As context grows, the team can apply application-level pruning, structured summaries, or provenance-preserving fact blocks, but those artifacts still have to be sent in the request that needs them. Prompt caching is useful when the same growing prefix is repeated, especially in multi-turn conversations, but it trades off cost and latency rather than changing the stateless API model. The official docs describe stateless multi-turn history in Using the Messages API , the input and output accumulation model in Context windows , and the caching behavior in Prompt caching . The Messages API is stateless, so Claude does not implicitly remember prior calls. Reconstructing the conversation history in each request gives the model the prior findings it needs to synthesize consistently.","domain":5,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q041","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. PR reviews are producing false positives because migration-specific review criteria are being applied to frontend and documentation changes. Migration files can appear under several packages, such as services, apps, and shared libraries. What change should this team make so those conventions are loaded only when Claude reads relevant files?","options":["Create .claude/rules/database-migrations.md without frontmatter and rely on its filename to target migration files.","Add .claude/rules/database-migrations.md with YAML frontmatter paths: [\"**/migrations/**/*.{sql,ts}\"] and place the migration review checklist there.","Move the migration checklist into the root CLAUDE.md and instruct Claude to apply it only to migration-related files.","Add ~/.claude/rules/database-migrations.md with the migration glob and ask each reviewer to install it locally."],"explanation":"For conventions that should apply only to certain file types or directories, Claude Code supports path-specific rules as Markdown files under .claude/rules/ . A rule becomes conditional by adding YAML frontmatter with a paths field, such as paths: [\"**/migrations/**/*.{sql,ts}\"] , and Claude loads it when it reads files matching those glob patterns. This is a good fit for CI review workflows because irrelevant review criteria can create false positives and reduce developer trust. Keeping specialized instructions out of always-loaded CLAUDE.md reduces context noise while still making the rules available when Claude inspects matching files. Path rules are still context , not hard enforcement. If the pipeline needed to block dangerous actions or enforce permissions, that would require mechanisms such as hooks or settings-based restrictions, but conditional convention loading belongs in project-scoped rule files under .claude/rules/ . See Claude Code memory for the documented rule file location, paths frontmatter behavior, and glob examples. This uses the documented path-specific rules mechanism: Markdown files under .claude/rules/ with a paths field in YAML frontmatter. The rule will load when Claude reads files matching the migration glob, which reduces irrelevant review criteria in unrelated pull requests.","domain":3,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q042","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. During evals, the synthesis phase is given access to two client tools: `analyze_content` for web search snippets and `analyze_content_docs` for uploaded PDFs. The model frequently calls the PDF-oriented tool on search results and the web-oriented tool on document excerpts. Both tool descriptions are nearly identical, saying only that the tool analyzes content and returns key findings. The team must keep separate web-snippet and PDF-specific analyzers exposed for separate output contracts and audit logging. What change would most effectively reduce the wrong-tool calls?","options":["Keep the tool names unchanged, add more examples to the synthesis prompt, and ask Claude to choose the correct analyzer carefully.","Force `tool_choice` to the document analyzer first, then let Claude call the web analyzer if the first result is insufficient.","Merge the analyzers into one generic tool, accept either web snippets or PDFs, and decide source handling inside the tool.","Rename the tools with source-specific names, rewrite each description with use and non-use boundaries, and update the dispatcher for the new names."],"explanation":"When two tools appear to do the same thing, Claude has little basis for choosing reliably. Tool definitions should make the intended use clear in the description , including when to use the tool, when not to use it, what inputs it expects, and what it returns . Anthropic's troubleshooting guidance identifies wrong-tool calls as a likely symptom of description ambiguity, with the recommended fix being to differentiate tools by usage boundaries, not just capabilities. Names also matter as a supporting signal, especially as a tool library grows across sources or services. A name such as extract_web_results or extract_document_findings communicates the source boundary more clearly than a generic analyze_content . For client tools, renaming is not just cosmetic: the application receives a tool_use block with a name , and the dispatcher must run the implementation associated with that name. tool_choice can force tool use, but it should not be used to paper over ambiguous tool design when the appropriate tool varies by input. Few-shot examples can help with judgment, but they are less direct than fixing the tool definitions that Claude uses during selection. See Anthropic's documentation on implementing tool use , tool use overview , troubleshooting tool use , and handling tool calls . Claude's tool selection is driven primarily by descriptions, so two tools with overlapping descriptions create predictable ambiguity. Source-specific names reduce confusion as the tool library grows, and updating the dispatcher is required because client tool execution is keyed by the returned tool name.","domain":2,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q043","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. Your CI review job needs a project-scoped HTTP MCP server for pull-request metadata. The server requires a bearer token in the Authorization header, and the MCP configuration should be shared with every checkout while avoiding committed credentials. Which implementation best satisfies this requirement?","options":["Commit a .mcp.json file that references Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN in headers, and rely on Claude Code to expand it during connection.","Commit a .mcp.json file that includes the bearer token directly in headers, and limit repository access to approved maintainers.","Commit a .mcp.json file that references Bearer ${GITHUB_TOKEN} in headers, and set GITHUB_TOKEN in each CI and developer environment.","Store the MCP server only in ~/.claude.json with the Authorization token, and document the manual setup for every contributor."],"explanation":"For team-shared MCP servers in Claude Code, .mcp.json at the project root is the project-scope configuration file intended to be checked into version control. The secure pattern is to commit the non-secret server shape, then reference machine-specific or sensitive values through environment variable expansion rather than storing the values themselves. Claude Code supports expansion using ${VAR} and ${VAR:-default} , including in headers for HTTP MCP servers. If a variable referenced without a default is not set, Claude Code fails to parse the configuration, so CI and developer environments must provide the variable before the server can connect. This is distinct from approval and authentication flows. Project-scoped MCP servers may still require user approval before use, and OAuth-based MCP authentication is a separate mechanism, not a fallback for a rejected static header token. See the official Claude Code MCP documentation for project scope, environment variable expansion syntax, supported fields, and related MCP behavior. Project-scoped MCP configuration belongs in .mcp.json at the project root when it should be shared through version control. Referencing ${GITHUB_TOKEN} in the headers field keeps the token out of the committed file while allowing Claude Code to expand it from each environment.","domain":2,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q044","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. The document-analysis subagent uses client-side tools to read licensed reports and extract evidence. When a report is unavailable, the tool returns a `tool_result` with `is_error: true`, but the content is always `\"Operation failed\"`, whether the cause is an expired license, a temporary timeout, an unsupported file format, or a valid search with no matching passages. The coordinator then either retries, drops the source, or escalates unpredictably. What change would best improve recovery behavior?","options":["Expand the document tool descriptions so Claude avoids tools that may fail.","Convert failed document reads into empty result sets so synthesis can continue.","Return categorized tool_result errors with retryability, cause, attempted input, and suggested recovery.","Add a coordinator instruction to retry every failed document tool call before escalating."],"explanation":"For client tools , Claude does not execute the external operation itself. Claude emits a structured tool_use request, the application runs the tool, and the application sends the result back in a tool_result block, as described in Tool use overview and Handle tool calls . Because the model only sees what the application returns, a generic message such as Operation failed removes the information needed for recovery. A timeout suggests retrying, a validation or format problem suggests changing the input or using a different parser, a permission problem suggests escalation, and a valid empty result should be treated as evidence absence rather than execution failure. The practical design pattern is to return structured, actionable error content in the tool_result , while preserving the link to the original tool_use_id . That gives the coordinator enough context to decide whether to retry, reformulate, route to another subagent, continue with a coverage-gap note, or escalate to a human reviewer. Claude can only reason from the tool result content your application returns for client-side tools. Categorized errors let the coordinator distinguish retryable failures, fixable inputs, permission problems, and valid empty results.","domain":2,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q045","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. A custom /parallel-review command starts an SDK run in a repository pinned to a pre-v2.1.63 Claude Code configuration where subagent delegation is performed with `Task`. It should fan out across specialized review subagents. The current SDK options auto-approve only Read, Grep, and Glob, and each run pauses at the first attempted subagent delegation with an interactive permission prompt. What should the team change?","options":["Add `Agent` only to worker subagents' `tools` lists for nested calls.","Add `Task` delegation guidance to `CLAUDE.md` for the project repository.","Add `Task` to the coordinator SDK run's `allowedTools` or `allowed_tools` list.","Remove delegation tools from `allowedTools` and rely on permission prompts."],"explanation":"For the legacy configuration described in the stem, subagent delegation is performed through Task , so the coordinator SDK run needs that delegation tool in allowedTools or allowed_tools . The practical issue is not that the subagents are undefined, it is that the first delegation attempt is not auto-approved and therefore falls into interactive permission handling. Current Claude Code documentation describes the renamed Agent tool for spawning subagents, and notes that Task was renamed to Agent in Claude Code v2.1.63. That version distinction matters: current configurations should generally use Agent , while a pinned legacy configuration that delegates with Task must auto-approve Task . Also remember that allowedTools is not a hard allowlist by itself. It auto-approves listed tools, while unlisted tools can still fall through to permission mode or a permission callback unless separately denied or locked down. See Sub-agents , Agent SDK subagents , and Agent SDK permissions . In this legacy configuration, subagent delegation is performed with `Task`, so the coordinator's SDK run must auto-approve that tool to avoid the permission prompt. Adding it to `allowedTools` or `allowed_tools` addresses the described pause during the first attempted delegation.","domain":1,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q046","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. The system described above uses one Claude call for pull request review, test generation, and PR metadata updates. Logs show the review phase sometimes invokes test-writing tools for non-testable style comments, then returns prose instead of the structured finding object your pipeline expects. What change best improves tool selection reliability while preserving automated test generation elsewhere in the pipeline?","options":["Separate review and test-generation steps, expose only role-specific tools to each step, and force the review step's reporting tool with tool_choice.","Expose all CI tools to both steps, set tool_choice to none during review, and parse JSON from assistant text.","Keep one shared CI step, namespace tool names by category, and leave tool_choice on auto for model flexibility.","Keep one shared CI step, expose all tools with sharper descriptions, and set tool_choice to any for review output."],"explanation":"When a workflow has distinct responsibilities, tool access should be scoped to the role performing that step. A review step should not have write-test or PR-label mutation tools if its job is to produce review findings, because every extra tool increases the chance of an irrelevant action being selected. In the Messages API, client tools are declared in the top-level tools parameter, and each tool definition includes a name , description , and input_schema . Anthropic emphasizes that tool descriptions are the primary steering mechanism for tool selection, but description quality is not a substitute for removing tools that are unrelated to the agent's current role. tool_choice is the control to use when the pipeline requires a tool call. any forces one of the available tools, while {\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":\"...\"} forces a specific named tool. For CI review output, forcing the review-reporting tool lets the pipeline receive structured findings while a separate test-generation step can keep its own file-writing tools. See Anthropic's tool-use documentation for the documented tools structure, tool description guidance, and tool_choice options: Implement tool use . This reduces the tool selection space during review and prevents review logic from reaching unrelated test-writing actions. Forcing the named reporting tool is the documented way to require a specific tool call when the pipeline needs structured output from that step.","domain":2,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q047","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. This team wants to move non-urgent post-resolution quality checks to the Message Batches API. Cases arrive continuously, and each check must reach a terminal batch outcome within 30 hours of case arrival for audit triage. Assume a batch may take the full documented 24-hour processing window before results are available or the batch expires. Which submission cadence should the team use?","options":["Submit accumulated requests once daily, using custom_id to correlate each case outcome.","Submit accumulated requests every 12 hours, using custom_id to correlate each case outcome.","Submit accumulated requests every 4 hours, using custom_id to correlate each case outcome.","Submit accumulated requests every 8 hours, using custom_id to correlate each case outcome."],"explanation":"The useful mental model for the Message Batches API is that it is an asynchronous processing mechanism for large volumes of Messages API requests, not a low-latency path for urgent work. For SLA planning, separate the time an item waits before submission from the time Anthropic may spend processing the batch. Here the SLA is 30 hours from item arrival, and the assumed batch-processing window is 24 hours. That leaves 30 - 24 = 6 hours as the maximum safe accumulation window before submission. A 4-hour cadence fits within that bound and leaves 2 hours of buffer, while 8 hours, 12 hours, and daily submission can exceed the SLA in the worst case. The 24-hour point should be treated carefully: the documentation describes when results become accessible or when a batch may expire, not a guarantee that every request succeeds within 24 hours. Each request should still use a valid, unique custom_id so downstream systems can correlate outcomes back to the originating support case. See Anthropic's Message Batches API documentation for the asynchronous processing model, batch limits, result availability behavior, and custom_id guidance. A 30-hour business SLA with a possible 24-hour batch processing window leaves at most 6 hours for pre-submission accumulation. Submitting every 4 hours keeps the worst-case timeline within the SLA and leaves a 2-hour buffer.","domain":4,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q048","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. Production traces show that every incoming request is sent through separate identity, order, refund-policy, and escalation-summary subagents. Simple order-status questions now have high latency, and some responses include irrelevant refund-policy caveats. What change best improves the design while preserving specialized workers for cases that need them?","options":["Route every case through all subagents, then ask the final responder to remove irrelevant findings before answering.","Give every subagent the same backend tools, then instruct each worker to skip itself when it is not needed.","Add a coordinator step that inspects the case, invokes only needed subagents, and passes each a self-contained task summary.","Replace the subagents with one large system prompt that covers identity, orders, refunds, and escalation."],"explanation":"A good coordinator does not treat every request as if it needs the maximum workflow. It should analyze the current case, determine which capabilities are actually needed, and delegate only the self-contained pieces of work that benefit from a specialized worker. Subagents are useful because they run in their own context window with their own instructions and tool access, then return results to the caller. That isolation is valuable for verbose investigations, but it also means the coordinator must pass the necessary facts explicitly rather than assuming the worker shares the main conversation context. The common anti-pattern is an always full pipeline : identity, order, refund, and escalation analysis all run even when the customer only needs a simple answer. This wastes latency and can introduce irrelevant caveats into the final response. A coordinator-level routing step preserves specialization while reducing unnecessary work and limiting tool exposure. Anthropic documents description-driven delegation, isolated subagent context, and tool restrictions for subagents in Claude Code sub-agents . For API-based tool orchestration, Claude can also decide on each turn whether to call tools under automatic tool choice, as described in Tool use overview . This design keeps the benefits of specialized subagents while avoiding unnecessary work on simple cases. Because subagents have isolated context, the coordinator must explicitly provide the relevant case facts and task scope to each worker it invokes.","domain":1,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q049","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a customer support resolution agent using the Claude Agent SDK. The agent handles high-ambiguity requests like returns, billing disputes, and account issues. It has access to your backend systems through custom Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools (get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund, escalate_to_human). Your target is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate. The team is refactoring long cases into a coordinator that can delegate billing and returns investigation steps. During testing, a container crash mid-dispute causes the replacement worker to lose the verified customer ID, prior order findings, and a decision that the case must be escalated. The workers do not share a filesystem, and local session JSONL files are not reliably present after restart. What change best supports safe crash recovery?","options":["Add stronger memory instructions to the system prompt, then restart the agent from the last customer message.","Copy local JSONL transcript files opportunistically, then resume whichever session appears newest on the replacement worker.","Resume the Agent SDK session with continue, then let the coordinator reconstruct state from the latest local transcript.","Persist an application-defined JSON state manifest, then load it on restart and inject it into coordinator and subagent prompts."],"explanation":"For crash recovery, distinguish conversation transcript from application state . Agent SDK sessions record the prompt, tool calls, tool results, and responses, but they are stored locally by path and host, so autoscaled workers that do not share a filesystem cannot rely on local JSONL availability. The safer pattern here is an application-defined state manifest, optionally produced with outputFormat or output_format using type: \"json_schema\" , that captures the verified customer, relevant order facts, actions already taken, pending escalation, and decisions the coordinator must honor. On recovery, the coordinator should load that durable manifest and include it explicitly in its own prompt and in any subagent delegation prompts. This avoids the common failure mode where a new agent has a transcript fragment or last user message but not the business-critical facts needed to avoid duplicate refunds or missed escalations. If the team does need transcript continuity across hosts, use documented mechanisms such as restoring the JSONL transcript to the same path and CWD or a SessionStore , while remembering that SessionStore mirrors transcripts and has best-effort write behavior rather than replacing durable application state. See Agent SDK Sessions , Session Storage , and Structured Outputs . This treats crash recovery facts as durable application state rather than assuming the next worker can access a local session file. Loading the JSON state manifest and injecting it into coordinator and subagent prompts makes the verified IDs, prior findings, and escalation decision explicit after restart.","domain":5,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q050","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. During evaluation, the synthesis subagent receives a long concatenation of subagent outputs. It repeatedly omits well-supported findings that appear midway through the bundle, while preserving findings near the start and end. What change would best reduce this failure while keeping the synthesis step grounded in the original evidence?","options":["Replace detailed evidence with progressively shorter summaries, then pass only the newest summary to the synthesis agent.","Prepend a concise findings map before the aggregated outputs, then group detailed evidence under explicit source and topic headers.","Concatenate subagent outputs in completion order, then ask the synthesis agent to infer the main themes from the full bundle.","Move all source URLs to a final appendix, then keep the narrative evidence together in one continuous section."],"explanation":"Long-context systems should not treat a large context window as a guarantee that every part of the input will receive equal attention. A practical mitigation is to put a key findings summary or findings map at the beginning of the aggregated input, then organize the supporting material with clear section boundaries so the synthesis agent can locate the relevant evidence. For a multi-agent research system, this pattern preserves both recall and provenance. The coordinator can present a front-loaded map of major claims, coverage gaps, conflicts, and source groupings, while the full subagent outputs remain available underneath explicit topic and source headers. This is different from compressing everything into a final summary, which can make the report less grounded and harder to cite. Anthropic guidance on long-context prompting emphasizes careful prompt organization and clear structure for large inputs. See Long context prompting tips for related guidance on making long inputs easier for Claude to use. This directly addresses position sensitivity by placing the most important synthesis cues where they are easier for the model to use. Explicit headers also make the detailed evidence easier to navigate without removing the provenance needed for cited reporting.","domain":5,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q051","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. This team has a custom /source-check slash command that asks Claude Code to verify report claims against cited sources. One engineer stored source-check.md under ~/.claude/commands/, and the command works for them but not for teammates who clone the repository. What should the team do to make the same command available from the repository?","options":["Add the source-check instructions to the root CLAUDE.md and invoke them manually.","Keep source-check.md in ~/.claude/commands/ and document the command in the project README.","Keep source-check.md in .claude/commands/ but exclude the .claude directory from version control.","Move source-check.md into .claude/commands/ in the repository and commit that file."],"explanation":"Claude Code distinguishes between project-scoped configuration under a repository’s .claude/ directory and personal configuration under ~/.claude . For legacy custom slash commands, a Markdown file such as .claude/commands/source-check.md defines the /source-check command for the current project, while ~/.claude/commands/source-check.md defines a personal command available to that user across projects. The practical design tradeoff is sharing versus personal customization. Project commands belong in the repository when a team wants a consistent workflow, such as a report source-checking prompt for a multi-agent research system. Personal commands are useful for an individual developer’s preferences, but they should not be used as the distribution mechanism for team-standard commands. Anthropic documents that the .claude directory contains project files intended to be committed and shared, while ~/.claude contains personal files. The legacy command format is still supported, even though Anthropic now recommends skills for newer reusable workflows. See Claude directory and Slash commands . Project-scoped legacy slash commands are stored under .claude/commands/ in the project. Because that directory can be committed with the repository, teammates who clone or pull the project can receive the same command definition.","domain":3,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q052","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. After one run has completed a broad source review and produced a useful baseline analysis, the team wants to compare two different synthesis strategies from that same starting point. They need each strategy exploration to keep its own conversational history without changing the baseline session history. What should the team do?","options":["Resume the baseline session with the SDK fork option enabled for each strategy, then track the new session IDs.","Enable file checkpointing for each strategy, then use checkpoints as the branching mechanism for session history.","Use continue in each strategy worker, then let the SDK select the latest session in the current directory.","Resume the baseline session normally for each strategy, then rely on separate prompts to keep the branches distinct."],"explanation":"For divergent research approaches, the important distinction is between conversation state and filesystem state. An Agent SDK session is the transcript of prompts, tool calls, tool results, and responses, so returning to a session restores the model's prior conversational context, such as the sources reviewed and analysis already performed. Forking is designed for the case where a team wants multiple branches from a shared analysis baseline. When used with a specific session ID, it creates a new session copied from the resumed session, gives the branch its own session ID, and leaves the original session history unchanged. Normal resume is not enough because it continues the original history. continue is even less precise because it finds the most recent session in the current directory, which can be the wrong baseline in a busy research workflow. Forking does not isolate file changes. If a forked strategy writes files in the same working directory, those file edits are real and visible to other sessions using that directory, so use file checkpointing or separate working directories when file-level isolation is also required. See Agent SDK sessions for the documented behavior of continue, resume, and fork. Forking while resuming a specific session creates a new session that starts with a copy of the original conversation history. This lets each synthesis strategy branch from the same analysis baseline while leaving the baseline session ID and history unchanged.","domain":1,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q053","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. This team is adding SDK-managed subagents for code review and documentation work. They want Claude to route work to the right subagent based on its purpose, and they want the documentation subagent to read files but not edit or run commands. Which configuration should they use?","options":["Define a programmatic agents map keyed by subagent type, set each description and prompt, and give the documentation subagent a read-only tools allowlist.","Define top-level allowedTools for read-only tools, add routing guidance to CLAUDE.md, and omit per-subagent tool settings.","Define agents by display name, put the subagent type inside prompt, and block editing tools through top-level disallowedTools.","Create subagent files with prompts only, use filenames as routing descriptions, and rely on parent permissions for tool limits."],"explanation":"For SDK-managed subagents, the central configuration object is the AgentDefinition inside the programmatic agents option. Each entry is keyed by the subagent type, and that key is what Claude uses when invoking the Agent tool with subagent_type . The required description is the natural-language routing cue, while the required prompt acts as the subagent's system prompt. The important tool-control distinction is that AgentDefinition.tools is a subagent-specific allowlist . If it is omitted, the subagent inherits all tools available from the parent, which is not appropriate when a documentation subagent should be read-only. By contrast, top-level allowedTools or allowed_tools auto-approves tools, but does not restrict Claude to only those tools. Programmatic subagents are the recommended approach for SDK applications, and filesystem-based agents are a separate alternative. For more detail, see Anthropic's Agent SDK subagents guide and the Agent SDK TypeScript reference . This uses the documented AgentDefinition pattern for SDK applications. The map key is the subagent type, description guides routing, prompt defines the subagent behavior, and tools restricts that subagent to only the listed tools.","domain":1,"answer":0},
  {"id":"pt5-q054","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. A payments platform team is about to migrate a shared logging library used across more than 45 files. Two viable integration approaches have different infrastructure requirements, and the maintainers want Claude Code to inspect the codebase and propose an approach before implementation begins. Which initial workflow is most appropriate?","options":["Start in bypassPermissions, let Claude run commands freely, and rely on tests to identify any regressions afterward.","Start in acceptEdits, let Claude apply the migration, and review the resulting diff after the changes finish.","Enter plan mode, have Claude inspect the affected areas, and review the proposed plan before choosing execution mode.","Start with a direct implementation prompt, ask Claude to choose an approach, and refine the design during editing."],"explanation":"Plan mode in Claude Code is a permission mode for analysis before implementation, not a different model or subscription tier. It lets Claude inspect the codebase and develop a plan while avoiding source file edits until the user approves the plan, which is exactly the control point needed for large migrations and architecture-sensitive changes. Anthropic's best-practices guidance recommends planning when the approach is uncertain, when many files may change, or when the developer needs exploration before committing to an implementation path. After Claude presents a plan, approving it exits plan mode and switches into a selected execution mode, such as reviewing each edit manually or accepting edits. For a typo, log line, or simple rename, this checkpoint can be unnecessary, but for a 45-plus-file migration with infrastructure implications, separating planning from editing reduces avoidable churn and makes the architectural decision explicit. See Claude Code permission modes and Claude Code best practices for the documented behavior and recommended use cases. Plan mode is designed to separate exploration and planning from implementation. It fits a large, uncertain migration because Claude can investigate the codebase and present a plan without editing source files until the plan is approved.","domain":3,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q055","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. During evaluation, final reports cite sources but sometimes treat a peer-reviewed study, a vendor benchmark, and an internal survey as equivalent evidence for the same claim. The search and document-analysis subagents currently return concise finding summaries plus a general list of sources consulted. What change best supports accurate downstream synthesis?","options":["Give each subagent broader tool access so it can inspect all available sources before summarizing its findings.","Ask the coordinator to retain full subagent transcripts and have the synthesizer infer provenance from prior work.","Add a report-generation pass that expands the final narrative with a separate bibliography and citation appendix.","Require each subagent to return structured claim records with source location, publication date, evidence excerpt, and methodology notes."],"explanation":"In a multi-agent research system, provenance is an interface contract . A subagent may perform a rich investigation, but downstream agents can only depend on the information that is explicitly returned in its summary or handoff. If the returned output says only that a source supports a finding, the synthesizer may lose the distinction between evidence types, time periods, source locations, and methods. Claude Code subagents are designed for context management : they handle work in their own context and return a summary to the main conversation. The official subagents documentation notes that non-fork subagents start with a fresh, isolated context and do not automatically share the parent conversation history, which makes explicit handoff content essential. See Claude Code subagents . The reliable pattern is to require structured claim records, for example: claim, evidence excerpt, source URL or document location, publication or collection date, and methodology notes. This is not just a citation formatting preference; it prevents the synthesis agent from treating incomparable evidence as interchangeable. Anthropic's tool-use guidance also emphasizes returning high-signal fields rather than bloated responses, which supports concise but complete provenance handoffs. See Implement tool use . This makes provenance and methodological context part of the handoff contract rather than something the synthesizer must reconstruct later. Because subagents return only what they provide back to the main conversation, the coordinator needs these fields preserved in the returned summary.","domain":5,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q056","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. Long pull requests are causing the review step to run near the context limit. The trace shows each file-analysis tool call appends a large tool_result payload with build metadata, ownership data, full lint output, historical CI runs, and dependency details, even though the reviewer uses only a small subset of those fields. What change best addresses the reliability issue?","options":["Enable context editing to clear old tool results after the request crosses a configured threshold.","Increase max_tokens for the review response and instruct Claude to ignore irrelevant tool-result fields.","Change the tool wrapper to return compact, review-specific tool_result payloads with only the fields needed.","Apply prompt caching to the tool definitions and repeated review prefix to reduce context-window pressure."],"explanation":"Tool-heavy review agents often fail because tool results become part of the conversation context . Anthropic documents that tool definitions and accumulated tool_result blocks can exhaust context in long-running agents, and the context window includes messages, tool results, tool definitions, and generated output. See Manage tool context and Context windows . The right mental model is that every field returned by a tool is something Claude must carry forward if it remains in messages . A 40-field payload is not harmless just because the prompt says only a few fields matter. The most reliable fix is to shape the tool output before creating the tool_result , returning only the review-relevant evidence needed for the next reasoning step. Other mechanisms solve different problems. Prompt caching can reduce billing and latency for repeated prefixes, but cached tokens still count toward the context window. Context editing can clear older tool results, but it is not a substitute for designing compact current tool outputs. The tool lifecycle itself is documented in Handle tool calls , where tool results are returned inside normal user messages rather than a separate tool role. This directly reduces the amount of tool output added to the conversation history. Since tool_result blocks count toward the context window, shaping results before they enter context preserves room for the review evidence that actually matters.","domain":5,"answer":2},
  {"id":"pt5-q057","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. A named dependency-impact subagent is used to inspect changed packages before a refactor plan is finalized. When a local test command intermittently times out after the subagent has already identified several affected files, the parent conversation receives only \"impact analysis failed,\" so the plan omits useful findings. What change best preserves resilience without flooding the parent conversation with raw exploration output?","options":["Move the dependency-impact inspection back into the parent conversation, then keep all command logs for planning.","Leave both tools and disallowedTools unset, then rely on inherited access to prevent further Bash retries.","Have the parent inspect the subagent's intermediate Bash calls, then rerun only the failed command before planning.","Have the subagent retry transient command failures locally, then return attempts, partial findings, and unresolved errors in its final result."],"explanation":"Claude Code subagents are specialized workers inside a single Claude Code session, each with its own context window, system prompt, tool access, and permissions. They are useful for dependency-impact scans because raw grep output, command logs, and file contents can stay out of the main conversation while the parent receives a compact result. The important reliability tradeoff is that the parent conversation receives one text result from the subagent, not a stream of the subagent's intermediate tool calls. If transient failures matter to the parent, the subagent must include recovery metadata in that final result, such as what commands were attempted, which failures were retried, what partial findings were preserved, and what uncertainty remains. Keeping the inspection in the parent conversation makes failures easier to see, but it sacrifices the context-isolation benefit that subagents are designed to provide. A better pattern is local recovery first, followed by structured propagation only when the subagent cannot fully resolve the failure. Learn more about subagent behavior in Claude Code subagents and the built-in spawning mechanism in the Claude Code tools reference . Claude Code subagents have their own context and return one text result to the parent conversation, so recovery details must be summarized by the subagent if the parent needs them. Retrying transient failures locally keeps noisy exploration inside the subagent while still giving the parent enough information to plan around remaining uncertainty.","domain":2,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q058","exam":"pt5","question":"You are using Claude Code to accelerate software development. Your team uses it for code generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation. You need to integrate it into your development workflow with custom slash commands, CLAUDE.md configurations, and understand when to use plan mode vs direct execution. In this team's monorepo, unit and component tests sit next to the source files they cover under services/, packages/, and apps/. Claude Code applies inconsistent testing guidance because similar instructions were copied into several directory-level memory files, and some directories are missing updates. What should you change to make the testing convention easier to maintain while avoiding unnecessary context for non-test work?","options":["Put the testing conventions in a project slash command under `.claude/commands/` and ask developers to run it before editing tests.","Move the testing conventions into the root `CLAUDE.md` so Claude loads them during all project conversations.","Add a `CLAUDE.md` file to every directory that contains tests and duplicate the shared testing conventions there.","Create a `.claude/rules/testing.md` file with `paths` globs for test files and store the shared testing conventions there."],"explanation":"When a convention applies to files scattered across a repository, use a path-scoped rule rather than many directory-local memory files. Claude Code supports markdown rules under .claude/rules/ with YAML frontmatter such as a paths field, allowing a rule to target glob patterns like test file names across multiple directories. This design trades a small amount of configuration structure for lower maintenance burden and less irrelevant context. A directory-level CLAUDE.md is tied to location, which works well for area-specific ownership, but scattered test files are better matched by file pattern than by directory. A root CLAUDE.md would be simpler, but it would load test-only guidance even when Claude is working on unrelated files. Remember that rules and CLAUDE.md files are still instructions in context , not hard enforcement. If the team must block or require behavior regardless of Claude's judgment, use hooks or settings-based permissions instead. For the documented rule-loading behavior, see Claude Code memory and the large-codebase guidance in Claude Code large codebases . Path-scoped rules are designed for conventions that apply to files matching patterns across many locations in a codebase. Keeping the testing guidance in one rule file avoids duplicated directory-level instructions and loads the rule only when Claude reads matching files.","domain":3,"answer":3},
  {"id":"pt5-q059","exam":"pt5","question":"You are building a multi-agent research system using the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents: one searches the web, one analyzes documents, one synthesizes findings, and one generates reports. The system researches topics and produces comprehensive, cited reports. After receiving a broad prompt such as \"produce a comprehensive cited report on AI adoption in healthcare,\" the coordinator must decide how to start before delegating research. Past runs produced well-written reports but had uneven coverage and wasted effort on tangential details. Which coordinator strategy best addresses this class of task?","options":["Start with a complete report outline from the report agent, then task other subagents with finding citations for each planned section.","Start with a discovery pass that maps the topic structure and high-impact areas, then assign prioritized subtasks that can change as findings reveal dependencies.","Start with parallel assignments for the most obvious keywords, then ask the synthesis agent to merge completed findings into a unified report.","Start with a fixed search-to-report pipeline, then prevent the coordinator from revising scope once the first subagent assignments are sent."],"explanation":"Open-ended work benefits from separating exploration , planning , and execution . In a multi-agent research system, exploration means mapping the topic dimensions, likely source types, stakeholder groups, and possible coverage gaps before the coordinator commits subagents to detailed work. Anthropic documents this practical pattern for agentic coding as Explore first, then plan, then code . The same mental model applies to research orchestration: acting too early can feel efficient, but it risks solving a narrow or incorrectly framed version of the task. Adaptive decomposition is the key tradeoff. A prioritized plan gives the system focus, while revision points let the coordinator adjust as subagents uncover dependencies, conflicting evidence, or missing domains. Broad parallel dispatch, rigid pipelines, and report-first workflows can all produce fluent outputs, but they are less reliable at achieving comprehensive coverage. Open-ended research benefits from separating exploration and planning from execution. A coordinator that first maps the problem space can create better subagent assignments and revise them when early findings expose gaps or dependencies.","domain":1,"answer":1},
  {"id":"pt5-q060","exam":"pt5","question":"You are integrating Claude Code into your Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The system runs automated code reviews, generates test cases, and provides feedback on pull requests. You need to design prompts that provide actionable feedback and minimize false positives. This team is designing a new automated reviewer for flaky-test risk. The requested behavior has unresolved product and workflow questions, including when comments are actionable, when they are noise, and what verification should prove. What should the team do before asking Claude Code to implement it?","options":["Run a delegated subagent via the Agent tool to ask AskUserQuestion prompts, then pass its transcript to the implementer.","Run an interactive planning session where Claude uses AskUserQuestion, writes a self-contained SPEC.md, then implement it from a fresh session.","Run direct execution with a detailed review checklist in the prompt, then adjust the workflow after the first CI failures.","Run a scheduled autonomous Claude Code job that infers missing requirements from recent pull request discussions."],"explanation":"For larger or ambiguous Claude Code work, the recommended pattern is to let Claude interview the developer before implementation. The documented workflow starts from a minimal feature prompt, asks Claude to use AskUserQuestion to explore technical implementation, UI or developer experience, edge cases, concerns, and tradeoffs, then writes the result to a self-contained SPEC.md . See Best practices for Claude Code . This is especially useful for CI review prompts because false positives often come from fuzzy criteria. A good spec should name relevant files and interfaces, state what is out of scope, and include an end-to-end verification step, so the implementation session has concrete acceptance criteria rather than vague intent. AskUserQuestion is a built-in Claude Code tool for asking multiple-choice clarifying questions, as documented in the Tools reference and Agent SDK user input guide . It should not be confused with autonomous scheduled work, which cannot ask clarifying questions, or with delegated subagents through the Agent tool, where AskUserQuestion is not currently available. This follows the documented interview workflow for larger features: start with minimal direction, let Claude ask targeted clarifying questions, and turn the answers into a self-contained spec. Starting a fresh session for implementation keeps the execution context focused on the approved spec rather than the exploratory conversation.","domain":3,"answer":1}
